					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2156983&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606595993" alt="psul" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2156983)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;" >psul</a></span> <span class="odate time_1508886041 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 Oct 2017 23:00</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3619348">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Articles on this site are about the writing, and if that writing entertained or impressed you.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's not 100% accurate, though. There are plenty of tales on this site that are entertaining and impressive writing, but if they were uploaded (unedited) in a mainlist SCP slot, they would be downvoted.</p>
<p>My point is: there are different expectations for different types of submissions to the site. &quot;Entertaining or impressive&quot; is (hopefully) <strong>necessary</strong> for success, but it is not <strong>sufficient</strong>.</p>
<p>In my example above of tales posted on the mainlist, one of the other necessary requirements - the standard mainlist format - is missing, and therefore downvotes accrue.</p>
<p>In the case of this article, I downvoted it because the Foundation's unserious attitude, and that of the demons, destroyed my suspension of disbelief. I'm fine with humour in mainlist articles (and have upvoted many examples), provided that suspension of belief remains largely intact. Suspension of disbelief in the shared setting is (for me) another necessary element of a successful mainlist skip.</p>
<p>That same suspension of disbelief is not necessary (in my mind) when it comes to -J articles, which serve a different purpose, and are assessed against different criteria. As such, I have in the past downvoted articles on the mainlist that I might upvote as -Js. I suspect that others apply a similar criteria, with similar results.</p>
<p>As such, I don't agree with you that we're looking to &quot;limit creativity&quot; by downvoting (some) humorous writing on the mainlist. We're simply assessing different subsets of articles on the site by different criteria. That's what the &quot;arbitrary classifications&quot; mean in the first place! Otherwise we could just have one big list of articles on the site, without differentiating mainlisters from tales from -Js from -EXs from GoI formats - and I don't think you're suggesting that.</p>

							</div>

								
					
